{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>No por tu justicia.</b> Es decir, no es por tu justicia que eres digno de poseer la tierra; pero de todos modos la generación que había de entrar en la tierra era una generación digna, y así encuentras que Él les dijo ״וְאַתֶּם הַדְּבֵקִים בַה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״ y so forth (Deuteronomy 4:4) — only they were not complete enough for the land to be given them on their own merit if not for the covenant of the fathers. And although the covenant of the fathers would not have availed to possess the land if they had been wicked — and the generation of the wilderness proves it — as the Raavad wrote in his commentary on the Mishnah, in chapter 2 of Eduyyot, where we learned: the father merits for the son, and so forth, and in the numbering of the generations before Him — and this is the end, as it says ״קוֹרֵא הַדּוֹרוֹת מֵרֹאשׁ״ (Isaiah 41:4), end quote. And the Raavad wrote, and this is his language: that He sees the generation that is worthy and rolls along with them until that generation, and so forth — like the generation of Joshua, and so forth, end quote. This shows that a worthy generation is also required. Nevertheless, their merit did not contain the measure by which they would deserve to inherit the land if not for the covenant of the fathers. Accordingly, the verse's saying <b>not by your righteousness</b> and so forth means: even though you are a complete congregation, there is no power in your merit to attain this thing. And He was emphatic in saying ״לֹא בְצִדְקָתְךָ״ to indicate that their righteousness did not avail even to assist, for the covenant of the fathers suffices; rather, what their good deeds accomplished was to ensure that the good would not be withheld from them." "<b>Por la maldad de… y para… que juró….</b> Y no se conformó con solo una de estas cosas, pues sin la maldad de las naciones HaShem no las habría desposeído — no de acuerdo con la justicia — y por la maldad sola Israel no habría sido digno de tomar su tierra. O es posible que el sentido de ״מוֹרִישָׁם״ sea destruirlas, y por eso la maldad era necesaria, pues sin la maldad HaShem no habría decretado sobre ellas (Deuteronomy 20:16) ״לֹא תְחַיֶּה כָּל נְשָׁמָה״."], :book "deuteronomy", :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :es, :chapter 9, :timestamp "2026-07-27T17:25:24.375393482Z", :translated-from :en, :model "glm-5.2"}